# ---- Hardware profiles (switch these 3 lines only) ----
# WHISPER_MODEL is a full model ID, never a short name: the transcription backend serves only
# models it has on disk under that exact ID, and answers 404 ("Model 'small' is not installed
# locally") for anything else — which dead-letters the transcribe job as TRANSCRIPTION_REJECTED.
# The worker downloads the model onto the backend the first time it starts and waits until it is
# there, so no manual step is needed — but a name that is not a real model ID stops the worker at
# startup with a line naming it. The deployment guide's "The transcription model" section says how
# to list the valid IDs.
#
# GTX 1050 (development):      WHISPER_MODEL=Systran/faster-whisper-small     WHISPER_COMPUTE_TYPE=int8     WHISPER_DEVICE=cuda
# RTX 4070 Ti Super (target):  WHISPER_MODEL=Systran/faster-whisper-large-v3  WHISPER_COMPUTE_TYPE=float16  WHISPER_DEVICE=cuda
# CPU fallback (large-model quality, slow, fine for asynchronous jobs):
#                              WHISPER_MODEL=Systran/faster-whisper-large-v3  WHISPER_COMPUTE_TYPE=int8     WHISPER_DEVICE=cpu
#
# macOS development (no GPU access inside Docker containers on a Mac!):
#   Default — the CPU image in the container (full stack parity):
#                              WHISPER_MODEL=Systran/faster-whisper-small     WHISPER_COMPUTE_TYPE=int8     WHISPER_DEVICE=cpu
#                              WHISPER_IMAGE_TAG=latest-cpu
#   Speed mode — Whisper natively with Metal (whisper.cpp --server / mlx-whisper) on the host;
#   disable the Whisper container and set this in the worker:
#                              WHISPER_BASE_URL=http://host.docker.internal:8080/v1
WHISPER_MODEL=Systran/faster-whisper-small
WHISPER_COMPUTE_TYPE=int8
WHISPER_DEVICE=cuda
WHISPER_IMAGE_TAG=latest-cuda

# ---- Transcription worker ----
# Anything speaking the OpenAI-compatible transcription API works here (ADR-005):
# the speaches container, a whisperX serving wrapper, or host-native whisper.cpp.
# WHISPER_BASE_URL=http://whisper:8000/v1
# WHISPER_API_KEY=unused
# The language a meeting is transcribed in is resolved per meeting: the choice made on the
# recording screen, then the user's default from their settings, then this, then detection.
# So this is the fallback for a recording nobody said anything about — leave it unset to have
# those recordings detected.
# WHISPER_LANGUAGE=de
# On startup the worker checks whether the backend has WHISPER_MODEL on disk, downloads it if it
# does not, and only then starts consuming jobs — a restart with the model already in the
# whisper-models volume costs one request. Set this to false for a backend that serves a baked-in
# model or where you install models yourself; the worker then only warns about what it finds.
# WHISPER_MODEL_AUTO_INSTALL=true
# Budget for that whole step, including waiting for the backend to come up. Raise it for large-v3
# on a slow line; exceeding it stops the worker instead of leaving it half-configured. It also
# sizes how long the transport waits for the download's response header, so this is the number
# that governs a long download — but it has to stay under 2147483647, the largest value a timer
# can hold, and the worker refuses to start above that rather than aborting instantly.
# WHISPER_MODEL_INSTALL_TIMEOUT_MS=2700000
# The silence filter (Silero VAD) is on by default: the backend transcribes only the parts
# of the recording that contain speech. Leave it on. A long speechless stretch — a room
# recorded before the meeting starts — otherwise makes Whisper lock onto a repeated phrase,
# and that loop contaminates the rest of the transcript. The trade-off is that audio the
# VAD hears as silence is never transcribed; timestamps stay relative to the original
# recording either way.
# WHISPER_VAD_FILTER=true
# WHISPER_TIMEOUT_MS=1800000
# Attempts before a retryable failure is moved to the dead-letter queue, and the
# base delay of the exponential backoff.
# WORKER_CONCURRENCY=1
# WORKER_RETRY_LIMIT=4
# WORKER_RETRY_DELAY_SECONDS=30
# WORKER_JOB_EXPIRE_SECONDS=7200

# ---- Summary backend (OpenAI-compatible chat completions, ADR-005) ----
# Switching providers is these three variables and nothing else.
SUMMARY_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
SUMMARY_API_KEY=sk-or-...
SUMMARY_MODEL=vendor/model-name
# Later, self-hosted, same code:
# SUMMARY_BASE_URL=http://lmstudio-host:1234/v1  (or vLLM/Ollama)
# SUMMARY_API_KEY=unused
# SUMMARY_MODEL=local-model
#
# Transcript budget per summary, in estimated tokens. docs/COST-MODEL.md assumes
# 12-15k input tokens per meeting hour; a longer recording keeps its head and
# tail and has the middle elided rather than costing an unbounded amount.
# SUMMARY_MAX_INPUT_TOKENS=14000
# SUMMARY_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=4000
# SUMMARY_TEMPERATURE=0.2
# SUMMARY_TIMEOUT_MS=180000
# Summaries in flight per process — network-bound, so higher than transcription.
# SUMMARY_CONCURRENCY=2
# Request response_format=json_object. Off by default: several self-hosted
# OpenAI-compatible servers reject the field, and the parser handles plain text.
# SUMMARY_JSON_MODE=false

# ---- Infrastruktur ----
POSTGRES_USER=quorum
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME
POSTGRES_DB=quorum
MINIO_ROOT_USER=quorum-admin
MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME
# MinIO built-in KMS key — required so the bucket can enforce default
# server-side encryption (ADR-001). Generate with:
#   echo "quorum-key:$(head -c 32 /dev/urandom | base64)"
# Back this value up: without it the stored audio cannot be decrypted.
MINIO_KMS_SECRET_KEY=quorum-key:CHANGE_ME_BASE64_32_BYTES
S3_BUCKET=recordings
# Server-side encryption requested for every recording object (ADR-001).
# The bucket also gets default SSE from the minio-init service.
S3_SSE=AES256
API_PORT=8080
MINIO_CONSOLE_PORT=9001
# Host ports used only by docker-compose.dev.yml, the override that publishes Postgres and the
# MinIO S3 API so a server started on the host can reach them. Change them if the defaults collide
# with services already running on the machine.
POSTGRES_PORT=5432
MINIO_PORT=9000

# ---- Recording endpoint ----
# Development only: read tenant/user from the x-quorum-tenant-id / x-quorum-user-id headers on
# the recording WebSocket instead of from the access token. Off unless explicitly enabled.
RECORDING_ALLOW_HEADER_AUTH=false

# ---- Abuse and cost protection (limits and quotas) ----
# The GPU worker is the expensive attack target: every recorded hour buys GPU time and model
# tokens (~0.10 €/meeting hour, see docs/COST-MODEL.md). The values below are the built-in defaults, so
# a fresh checkout behaves identically whether or not they are set. All of them sit far above what
# a real meeting does. In V1 they apply to every user alike; plan tiers will vary them per user.
# Seconds of RECORDED AUDIO per session, after which the server finalizes the recording itself.
# Audio time, not wall clock: a paused recording spends none of this, so a workshop with a long
# break keeps its full allowance. This is the limit that tracks cost. 14400 = 4 hours of audio.
RECORDING_MAX_RECORDED_SECONDS=14400
# Wall-clock seconds a session may stay open, pauses included. Protects the open session itself —
# socket, server state, parallel-session slot — rather than the cost of the audio, which is why it
# sits far above the recorded-duration limit. 43200 = 12 hours.
RECORDING_MAX_SESSION_LIFETIME_SECONDS=43200
# Wall-clock seconds a single pause may last. Past it the session is FINALIZED, not killed: the
# meeting is complete and playable, and picking the work back up starts a new one. 7200 = 2 hours.
RECORDING_MAX_PAUSE_SECONDS=7200
# Recording sessions one user may have open at the same time (counted per API process).
RECORDING_MAX_PARALLEL_SESSIONS=3
# Sustained chunk frames per second, per connection. A live recording sends 0.5–1.
RECORDING_MAX_CHUNKS_PER_SECOND=20
# Sustained bytes per second, per connection. Live Opus is around 4 KiB/s. 4194304 = 4 MiB.
RECORDING_MAX_BYTES_PER_SECOND=4194304
# Seconds' worth of both rates above that one connection may spend at once — the head room a
# reconnecting client needs to replay the audio it buffered while offline.
RECORDING_RATE_BURST_SECONDS=10
# Total stored audio per user, in bytes. 53687091200 = 50 GiB, roughly 3,000 hours of Opus.
QUOTA_STORAGE_BYTES=53687091200
# Seconds one user may record per calendar month (UTC). 360000 = 100 hours.
QUOTA_MONTHLY_RECORDED_SECONDS=360000
# Chunks between two usage writes during a recording, per session. Lower means a crash loses less
# of the quota accounting, higher means fewer database writes. 64 is one to two minutes of audio.
QUOTA_USAGE_FLUSH_CHUNKS=64
# REST requests one user may make per window, across the whole API.
API_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=300
# Length of that window, in seconds.
API_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=60
# Requests to the summary regenerate route per window, per user. Its own, much smaller number
# because every accepted request there buys a model call.
API_RATE_LIMIT_SUMMARY_MAX=10

# ---- Auth (OIDC via Keycloak, ADR-006 §7) ----
# Keycloak runs inside the compose stack and imports infra/keycloak/realm-quorum.json on first
# start, so `docker compose up` gives a working login with no clicking in the admin console.
KEYCLOAK_VERSION=26.5
KEYCLOAK_PORT=8081
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN=admin
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME
# Keycloak's own logical database on the shared Postgres instance (no extra container).
KEYCLOAK_DB_NAME=keycloak
KEYCLOAK_DB_USER=keycloak
KEYCLOAK_DB_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME
# Origin at which browsers reach Keycloak; ends up in the `iss` claim of the tokens.
KEYCLOAK_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:8081
# Service account the API uses to give a self-registered account its own tenant on first sign-in.
# Every data object in Quorum is tenant-scoped (ADR-001), and Keycloak cannot compute a per-user
# attribute at registration time, so the API writes it — see infra/keycloak/README.md.
# The value below is the one the DEVELOPMENT realm declares. It is a committed fixture like the
# dev.* passwords, not a secret. A deployment MUST set its own; the preflight refuses to start
# without one. Generate it like any other: openssl rand -hex 24
KEYCLOAK_PROVISIONER_CLIENT_ID=quorum-provisioner
KEYCLOAK_PROVISIONER_SECRET=dev-provisioner-secret

# Server-side token validation. The internal issuer is used for discovery/JWKS inside the compose
# network; the public issuer is what browser-obtained tokens actually carry. Both are accepted as
# `iss`. When running the server outside compose, point both at the public URL.
OIDC_ISSUER_URL=http://keycloak:8080/realms/quorum
OIDC_PUBLIC_ISSUER_URL=http://localhost:8081/realms/quorum
# Public PWA client — Authorization Code + PKCE, no secret.
OIDC_CLIENT_ID=quorum-pwa
# Audience the API requires in the access token (added by the token mappers on the realm client).
OIDC_AUDIENCE=quorum-api
# Token claim carrying the tenant. Every data object is tenant/user scoped (ADR-001).
OIDC_TENANT_CLAIM=tenant_id

# ---- Mail, development ----
# The development stack runs a mailpit container and the development realm sends every message to
# it, so password reset is walkable end to end without a relay and without mail reaching anyone.
# Open the inbox at http://localhost:8025.
MAILPIT_VERSION=v1.27
MAILPIT_UI_PORT=8025
# Messages kept in memory before the oldest are dropped.
MAILPIT_MAX_MESSAGES=500

# ---- Server (@quorum/server) ----
HOST=0.0.0.0
PORT=8080
LOG_LEVEL=info

# =============================================================================================
# ---- Release deployment ----
#
# Everything above configures the development stack. The variables below are read by
# docker-compose.release.yml (CPU) or docker-compose.release-gpu.yml (GPU) — pick one file, not
# both. Together with this file that is the entire deployment: every script, every configuration
# file and the production realm are baked into the images, so there is nothing to clone or mount.
#
# The full walkthrough is at
# https://github.com/joCur/quorum/blob/main/docs/deployment.md
#
# MANDATORY — the stack refuses to start while any of these is missing or still holds a
# placeholder value, and tells you which:
#
#   POSTGRES_USER             KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD   MINIO_ROOT_USER
#   KEYCLOAK_PROVISIONER_SECRET
#   POSTGRES_PASSWORD         KEYCLOAK_PUBLIC_URL       MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD
#   POSTGRES_DB               QUORUM_PUBLIC_URL         MINIO_KMS_SECRET_KEY
#   KEYCLOAK_DB_PASSWORD      S3_BUCKET                 SUMMARY_BASE_URL
#   SUMMARY_API_KEY           SUMMARY_MODEL
#
# Passwords must be at least 16 characters. Generate each one separately:
#   openssl rand -hex 24
# Hex rather than base64 on purpose: POSTGRES_PASSWORD is embedded in the DATABASE_URL the
# compose file assembles, and a "/" or "@" in it silently truncates that URL. The preflight
# rejects a Postgres password containing a URL-reserved character for exactly that reason.
# =============================================================================================

# The origin browsers use. Must be https:// and not a loopback address — the stack rejects both.
# TLS is terminated in your own reverse proxy in front of this stack.
QUORUM_PUBLIC_URL=https://quorum.example.com

# Set KEYCLOAK_PUBLIC_URL (in the Auth section above) to the SAME origin: the edge proxy serves
# Keycloak under /realms on the app's own host, so the issuer in every token is
# https://quorum.example.com/realms/quorum. Point them at different hosts only if you also build
# your own client image — see the deployment guide, "Configuring the client image".
#
# The remaining mandatory values are declared once, in the sections above — set them there rather
# than adding a second copy here, because a duplicate key later in this file silently wins:
#
#   POSTGRES_USER / _DB / _PASSWORD      the domain database
#   KEYCLOAK_DB_PASSWORD                 Keycloak's own role on that database
#   KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD              used on EVERY deploy, not only the first
#   KEYCLOAK_PROVISIONER_SECRET          the API's service account for new-account setup
#   MINIO_ROOT_USER / _PASSWORD          object storage, also the S3 credentials
#   MINIO_KMS_SECRET_KEY                 storage encryption master key — BACK THIS UP
#   S3_BUCKET                            e.g. recordings
#   SUMMARY_BASE_URL / _API_KEY / _MODEL the summary backend (ADR-005)
#   WHISPER_MODEL / _DEVICE / _IMAGE_TAG cuda + latest-cuda for the GPU file, cpu + latest-cpu
#                                        for the CPU one. WHISPER_MODEL is a full model ID; the
#                                        worker installs it on first start — see the guide.

# ---- Mail delivery ----
# OFF by default, and off is a complete, working configuration: users sign in with the password
# they were given and nothing in the interface promises a mail that cannot arrive.
#
# One switch governs the whole feature. While it is false the realm sets resetPasswordAllowed to
# false, so the sign-in page shows no "Forgot password?" link — a door that opens onto a mail
# nobody can send is worse than no door. Turning it on makes every SMTP_* value below mandatory:
# the preflight refuses to start the stack on a missing or placeholder relay password, so the
# failure is one readable line at deploy time rather than a reset mail that vanishes.
#
# There is no mail container in this stack. Point it at your own relay — a provider's SMTP
# endpoint, or a smarthost on the same machine.
# QUORUM_SMTP_ENABLED=false
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# The sender address. Your relay has to be allowed to send as it, and so does your SPF record.
# SMTP_FROM=quorum@example.com
# SMTP_FROM_DISPLAY_NAME=Quorum
# Optional; leave empty unless you have a reason.
# SMTP_REPLY_TO=
# SMTP_ENVELOPE_FROM=
# Transport. STARTTLS on port 587 is the usual pair; SMTP_SSL=true is implicit TLS on port 465.
# Both false sends mail in the clear and the preflight says so.
# SMTP_SSL=false
# SMTP_STARTTLS=true
# SMTP_AUTH=true
# SMTP_USER=quorum@example.com
# SMTP_PASSWORD=

# ---- Optional ----
# Which release to run. Images default to `latest`, so `docker compose pull` moves you to the
# current release; set this to pin one instead. Updating is one command either way — pinning buys
# a deployment that is reproducible.
# QUORUM_VERSION=1.0.0
# Where the images live. Change it only when mirroring them into another registry.
# QUORUM_IMAGE_REGISTRY=ghcr.io/jocur

# Address the edge proxy is published on — the stack's only published port. Loopback by default,
# because your TLS-terminating proxy runs on the host and nothing else should reach plain HTTP.
# The API, Keycloak, Postgres and MinIO are not published at all, at any address.
# BIND_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1
# EDGE_PORT=8080

# Per-container resource limits. The defaults suit a single-host deployment; raise them on a
# bigger machine rather than removing them.
# EDGE_CPU_LIMIT=1           EDGE_MEMORY_LIMIT=128m
# CLIENT_CPU_LIMIT=1         CLIENT_MEMORY_LIMIT=128m
# API_CPU_LIMIT=2            API_MEMORY_LIMIT=1g
# WORKER_CPU_LIMIT=2         WORKER_MEMORY_LIMIT=2g
# KEYCLOAK_CPU_LIMIT=2       KEYCLOAK_MEMORY_LIMIT=1g
# POSTGRES_CPU_LIMIT=2       POSTGRES_MEMORY_LIMIT=2g
# MINIO_CPU_LIMIT=2          MINIO_MEMORY_LIMIT=2g
# WHISPER_CPU_LIMIT=4        WHISPER_MEMORY_LIMIT=8g

# Third-party image tags, pinned per deployment.
# MINIO_VERSION=latest

# Only needed with the opt-in `monitoring` profile, which has no default password.
# GRAFANA_ADMIN_USER=admin
# GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
